  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n1 1 2 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n4 1 1 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n4 4 1 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\nOutput:\n1 1 2 5 4 4 1 5 1 4 4\n4 1 1 5 4 1 1 5 1 1 4\n4 4 1 5 1 1 2 5 2 1 1\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n6 3 3 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n6 3 3 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n6 3 2 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\nOutput:\n6 3 3 5 6 6 6 5 2 3 6\n6 3 3 5 3 3 3 5 3 3 6\n6 3 2 5 2 3 3 5 3 3 6\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n2 7 8 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n7 7 8 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n8 8 8 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\nOutput:\n2 7 8 5 8 7 2 5 8 8 8\n7 7 8 5 8 7 7 5 8 7 7\n8 8 8 5 8 8 8 5 8 7 2\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n3 3 9 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n9 9 9 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n2 9 9 5 0 0 0 5 0 0 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    # Identify separator columns (all values are 5)\n    sep_cols = [c for c in range(cols) if all(grid[r][c] == 5 for r in range(rows))]\n    if len(sep_cols) < 2:\n        # No transformation possible, return a copy\n        return [row[:] for row in grid]\n    c1, c2 = sep_cols[0], sep_cols[1]\n    # Extract the left pattern (before first separator)\n    left = [row[:c1] for row in grid]\n    # Rotation helpers\n    def rotate90(m):\n        # 90 degrees clockwise\n        return [list(row) for row in zip(*m[::-1])]\n    def rotate180(m):\n        # 180 degrees\n        return [row[::-1] for row in m[::-1]]\n    # Compute rotated versions\n    middle = rotate90(left)\n    right = rotate180(left)\n    # Reassemble the grid\n    result = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        new_row = left[r] + [5] + middle[r] + [5] + right[r]\n        result.append(new_row)\n    return result",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    if not grid:\n        return []\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    # Identify separator columns (all values are 5)\n    sep_cols = [c for c in range(cols) if all(grid[r][c] == 5 for r in range(rows))]\n    if len(sep_cols) < 2:\n        # No transformation possible, return a copy\n        return [row[:] for row in grid]\n    c1, c2 = sep_cols[0], sep_cols[1]\n    # Extract the left pattern (before first separator)\n    left = [row[:c1] for row in grid]\n    # Rotation helpers\n    def rotate90(m):\n        # 90 degrees clockwise\n        return [list(row) for row in zip(*m[::-1])]\n    def rotate180(m):\n        # 180 degrees\n        return [row[::-1] for row in m[::-1]]\n    # Compute rotated versions\n    middle = rotate90(left)\n    right = rotate180(left)\n    # Reassemble the grid\n    result = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        new_row = left[r] + [5] + middle[r] + [5] + right[r]\n        result.append(new_row)\n    return result",
